        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          OBJECTIVES:<BR>
That astronauts will be exposed to the space radiation environment is unavoidable, and all radiation exposure has the potential to elicit a carcinogenic response. Cancer is characterized by gross destabilization of the genome. In vivo and in vitro exposure to radiation can lead to genomic instability. This radiation induced genomic instability shares many of the characteristics associated with genetic instabilities observed in human tumors and may well be process that transforms a normal cell to a neoplastic growth following radiation exposure. We have developed a rapid and sensitive assay for measuring induced genomic instability occurring in the progeny of irradiated cells. One way to protect space travelers from the deleterious effects of radiation exposure is with the use of radioprotective compounds. In this application we will investigate the radioprotective effect of three non-protein thiol drugs already approved for clinical use. 
<P>
This experiment investigated three specific aims: <br>
Specific aim 1 tested the hypothesis that non-protein thiols can reduce the frequency of genomic instability induced by High Z particles in human cells. These human cells were transfected with a sensitive and reliable reporter system to analyze delayed recombination/deletion events associated with radiation exposure in the progeny of surviving cells. <br>
Specific aim 2 tested the hypothesis that some of the delayed effects of high Z radiation result from nontargeted bystander effects resulting from soluble factors secreted by irradiated cells. <br>
Specific aim 3 investigated the molecular mechanism(s) by which the non-protein thiols exerted their protective effect. Specifically, the hypothesis was tested that treating cells with these drugs induced elevated intracellular levels of manganese superoxide dismutase and this reduced iron ion induced genomic instability in RKO36 cells. 
<p>
The goal of these studies were to determine whether drugs already approved for use in the human population can reduce genomic instability induced by radiations commonly encountered in space. If so, then these drugs can potentially be taken by astronauts to protect against deleterious effects associated with space radiations. 
<P>
APPROACH:<BR>
The approach for this experiment is not available.
<p>
RESULTS:<br>
Preliminary experiments using novel radiation countermeasures were conducted. These compounds are of particular interest in that they are selectively more cytotoxic to normal MCF10A breast cells than MCF7 breast cancer cells. These compounds selectively sensitize the tumor cells to radiation induced cell killing. Further, it should be noted that two of these novel compounds is approximately as effective as WR-1065 at protecting normal cells from the effects of a 4 Gy dose of radiation, while the cancer cells are at increased susceptibility to radiation induced cell killing. The final results for this experiment were submitted to NASA in a Final Report.
        </div>

